## Processing cancer data {#sec-process_cancer_data}

Now we're ready to write a function to process one of the cancer CSV files you have been provided with. 

First, let's just look at one dataset, `data/tcga_data/Brain_Lower_Grade_Glioma.csv`, without using a function.


:::{.callout-exercise #ex-cancer_no_function}
In your `TCGA_data_analysis.qmd` Quarto file, write a series of steps (not as a function) to:

* Read the file `data/tcga_data/Brain_Lower_Grade_Glioma.csv` into a data frame variable.
* Extract the column `Gene_ID` using the function `pull()` and store it in a variable, `gene_ids`.

::: {.callout-note .copycode #copy-libs_tcga}
### Include this code

Include this code at the top of your `TCGA_data_analysis.qmd` Quarto file.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(tidyverse)
library(gt)
```
:::

:::

:::{.callout-answer #ex-cancer_no_function_ans}

* Read the file `data/tcga_data/Brain_Lower_Grade_Glioma.csv` into a data frame variable.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
gene_mutation_counts <- read_csv("data/tcga_data/Brain_Lower_Grade_Glioma.csv")
```
:::


Extract the column `Gene_ID` using the function `pull()` and store it in a variable, `gene_ids`.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
gene_ids <- gene_mutation_counts |> pull(Gene_ID)
```
:::


:::
:::

Now, let's combine these steps into a function, which can be run on any of the cancer data frames.

We can add an argument, `cancer_type` to tell the function which cancer type we want to look at.

The `paste0` function is useful to combine the `cancer_type` variable with the path to our input file.
.

First, let's write a function which just reads the file.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
process_cancer_df <- function(cancer_type) {
  # Read the CSV file
  gene_mutation_counts <- read_csv(paste0("data/tcga_data/", cancer_type, ".csv"))
}
```
:::


We can then add the second step to do all our data frame processing within this function.

:::{.callout-exercise #ex-cancer_function}

In your `TCGA_data_analysis.qmd` Quarto file, convert your existing code into a function named `process_cancer_df` to carry out all the steps from [Exercise 1](@ex-cancer_no_function) and then **return** the value of `gene_ids`.

:::{.callout-answer collapse="true" #ex-cancer_function_ans}


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
process_cancer_df <- function(cancer_type) {
  # Read the CSV file
  gene_mutation_counts <- read_csv(paste0("data/tcga_data/", cancer_type, ".csv"))

  # Extract gene IDs
  gene_ids <- gene_mutation_counts |> pull(Gene_ID)

  # Return gene_ids variable
  return(gene_ids)
}
```
:::


:::
:::

